WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:16.430
&gt;&gt; We have one more
speaker for today,

00:00:16.431 --> 00:00:18.400
I'm very excited to
introduce to you,

00:00:18.401 --> 00:00:20.020
and that is Albert Greenberg.

00:00:20.021 --> 00:00:23.665
Albert is the Corporate Vice
President at Microsoft.

00:00:23.666 --> 00:00:25.720
He's in the Azure
Networking Group which is

00:00:25.721 --> 00:00:29.209
the team that develops engineers,

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:31.450
and runs all of the systems and

00:00:31.451 --> 00:00:34.230
tools that power
the Microsoft Cloud Network.

00:00:34.231 --> 00:00:39.310
He is well-known as an author
and a researcher himself.

00:00:39.311 --> 00:00:40.840
Multiple awards including

00:00:40.841 --> 00:00:42.990
a SIGMETRICS Test of Time Award.

00:00:42.991 --> 00:00:46.070
He's a veteran of
both Bell Labs and AT&amp;T,

00:00:46.071 --> 00:00:49.550
where he was a principal
in developing a lot of

00:00:49.551 --> 00:00:53.210
the systems and tools that
AT&amp;T used to run its network,

00:00:53.211 --> 00:00:55.790
and has done foundational work in

00:00:55.791 --> 00:00:57.710
developing the architectures and

00:00:57.711 --> 00:00:59.965
systems that are part of
software-defined networking.

00:00:59.966 --> 00:01:02.585
So, Albert joined us in 2007.

00:01:02.586 --> 00:01:04.465
He joined the Azure team,

00:01:04.466 --> 00:01:07.240
I should say Microsoft
Research in 2007.

00:01:07.241 --> 00:01:09.660
He joined the Azure team in 2010,

00:01:09.661 --> 00:01:11.145
and he's here with us tonight.

00:01:11.146 --> 00:01:14.550
So with that, thank you for
joining, Albert Greenberg.

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:21.500
&gt;&gt; Thank you. Hi. So, it's

00:01:21.501 --> 00:01:23.600
a pleasure to talk to
you about startups.

00:01:23.601 --> 00:01:27.635
The startup that we're on
is doubling every year,

00:01:27.636 --> 00:01:30.000
and it's an amazing journey.

00:01:30.001 --> 00:01:33.100
By the way, thank you for coming.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:40.290
I see a lot of faces
that I recognized in

00:01:40.291 --> 00:01:45.040
the audience and we have
a beautiful new space.

00:01:45.041 --> 00:01:47.110
You'll find that we're open

00:01:47.111 --> 00:01:49.160
and I'd like to see you more

00:01:49.161 --> 00:01:51.515
often than just in this room,

00:01:51.516 --> 00:01:53.650
and hopefully, spend
more time with you.

00:01:53.651 --> 00:01:59.600
So, I'm going to try and

00:01:59.601 --> 00:02:02.375
describe the challenge
of building a Cloud

00:02:02.376 --> 00:02:06.850
and the expectations that
we have for the Cloud.

00:02:06.851 --> 00:02:08.330
It's really exciting to

00:02:08.331 --> 00:02:09.930
be on this journey
to build the Cloud.

00:02:09.931 --> 00:02:13.860
We see it as the platform for
all the world's computing.

00:02:13.861 --> 00:02:17.720
It's a very inspiring task
to build and we see

00:02:17.721 --> 00:02:20.150
so much workload pouring on at

00:02:20.151 --> 00:02:24.960
mission-critical workloads,
hospitals, police forces.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:30.440
Interacting with groups like that

00:02:30.441 --> 00:02:33.515
where they take reliability
super seriously

00:02:33.516 --> 00:02:35.330
inspires us to even take

00:02:35.331 --> 00:02:37.010
it more seriously
and understand what

00:02:37.011 --> 00:02:41.285
they mean by reliability and
then embody that in our DNA.

00:02:41.286 --> 00:02:44.600
So, I'm going to talk about
reliability and convince you,

00:02:44.601 --> 00:02:46.070
I hope, that reliability is

00:02:46.071 --> 00:02:48.890
the coolest thing that
you could work on.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:54.730
Let's see, is that go?
Okay. So, one thing that at

00:02:54.731 --> 00:02:56.920
Microsoft we do and

00:02:56.921 --> 00:02:58.735
I do in every one of
my staff meetings,

00:02:58.736 --> 00:03:01.030
and every one of
our technical meetings

00:03:01.031 --> 00:03:04.640
