00:14:10.251 --> 00:14:11.545
So, you want agility,

00:14:11.546 --> 00:14:14.860
you want to be able to- we,

00:14:14.861 --> 00:14:16.950
in the cloud we ship weekly.

00:14:16.951 --> 00:14:19.920
When there's a bug we take
care of the bug right away

00:14:19.921 --> 00:14:25.355
within hours or as fast as
we can roll it out bug fix.

00:14:25.356 --> 00:14:27.735
It has to be serviceable.

00:14:27.736 --> 00:14:29.055
There's something that I don't

00:14:29.056 --> 00:14:31.070
know if academia studies enough.

00:14:31.071 --> 00:14:33.920
Serviceability it's so critical.

00:14:33.921 --> 00:14:35.850
You have to be able to update,

00:14:35.851 --> 00:14:41.510
it goes with reliability
and the velocity.

00:14:41.511 --> 00:14:43.590
You have to be able to
update without anybody

00:14:43.591 --> 00:14:46.210
noticing you have to be able
to update all the time.

00:14:46.211 --> 00:14:47.959
Things have to be modular,

00:14:47.960 --> 00:14:51.310
so you can do these Hitler's
upgrades and restart.

00:14:51.311 --> 00:14:55.140
It has to be as simple as use

00:14:55.141 --> 00:15:01.750
a normal sort of container
update to reload the OS image.

00:15:01.751 --> 00:15:04.370
It has to be something
you can already

00:15:04.371 --> 00:15:08.340
do and reliable up which
I've talked about.

00:15:08.341 --> 00:15:14.275
So, two things here,

00:15:14.276 --> 00:15:17.220
one is, we're getting

00:15:17.221 --> 00:15:18.955
a lot of help from
the industry so there are

00:15:18.956 --> 00:15:20.770
these companies that are

00:15:20.771 --> 00:15:23.620
producing amazing
merchants silicon.

00:15:23.621 --> 00:15:29.465
These are ASICs that

00:15:29.466 --> 00:15:31.240
are fairly cheap and

00:15:31.241 --> 00:15:33.385
can do everything you'd
like to do in networking,

00:15:33.386 --> 00:15:35.510
they have super high speed,

00:15:35.511 --> 00:15:36.520
they have lots of ports,

00:15:36.521 --> 00:15:37.770
they have the right kind
of buffering,

00:15:37.771 --> 00:15:41.960
they have incredible
capabilities hidden.

00:15:41.961 --> 00:15:44.355
You may not know about
them but the ASIC supports

00:15:44.356 --> 00:15:46.480
them and if you can
just get access to

00:15:46.481 --> 00:15:52.520
that ASIC and at the same time
you want to protect,

00:15:52.521 --> 00:15:54.040
you want to protect your software

00:15:54.041 --> 00:15:55.270
that runs above that ASICs,

00:15:55.271 --> 00:15:58.260
you need some sort of hardware
abstraction layer that

00:15:58.261 --> 00:16:01.685
allows you to plug
in any ASIC below,

00:16:01.686 --> 00:16:03.980
your software keeps working.

00:16:03.981 --> 00:16:05.835
But you can still get to

00:16:05.836 --> 00:16:09.045
the unique features
of the basic below.

00:16:09.046 --> 00:16:10.650
We have something called switch

00:16:10.651 --> 00:16:12.290
abstraction interface
which provides

00:16:12.291 --> 00:16:15.775
that and I'll tell you
it's really working out.

00:16:15.776 --> 00:16:19.380
So we can we don't have to go
with just one ASIC vendor,

00:16:19.381 --> 00:16:21.910
we can go with several.

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:27.300
It's again Open-Source,
go to GitHub, pick it up.

00:16:27.301 --> 00:16:29.400
It's Open-Source,

00:16:29.401 --> 00:16:32.520
it's supported through
the Open Compute Platform.

00:16:32.521 --> 00:16:37.875
Provides essentially the.H
files for your code.

00:16:37.876 --> 00:16:40.860
Common APIs which are then

00:16:40.861 --> 00:16:44.525
implemented by drivers beneath
it depending on the ASIC.

00:16:44.526 --> 00:16:47.620
It supports disaggregation
you don't have to buy

00:16:47.621 --> 00:16:49.910
this monolith you can get
the ASIC by the board,

00:16:49.911 --> 00:16:51.410
get the whole thing

00:16:51.411 --> 00:16:54.060
manufactured and build
your own router.

00:16:54.061 --> 00:16:57.125
And so, the software and

00:16:57.126 --> 00:17:00.630
hardware can evolve independently

00:17:00.631 --> 00:17:03.505
and there's an open
community around it.

00:17:03.506 --> 00:17:06.920
So, above this SAI,

00:17:06.921 --> 00:17:09.770
we have something
which was definitely

00:17:09.771 --> 00:17:11.780
named in a different way
so you would

00:17:11.781 --> 00:17:13.920
remember it called SONiC,

00:17:13.921 --> 00:17:17.150
and that's the software
that really operates

00:17:17.151 --> 00:17:21.015
the switch and that's
what we use in Azure.

00:17:21.016 --> 00:17:24.470
So, it's also
available through OCP,

00:17:24.471 --> 00:17:25.750
it provides everything you

00:17:25.751 --> 00:17:28.410
need in terms of functionality.

00:17:28.411 --> 00:17:30.405
I'll tell you
a little bit more but

00:17:30.406 --> 00:17:31.890
it really allows you to

00:17:31.891 --> 00:17:38.695
run your network devices like
you'd run a Cloud service,

00:17:38.696 --> 00:17:41.465
like you'd run
software in general,

00:17:41.466 --> 00:17:42.845
yet you can do networking.

00:17:42.846 --> 00:17:45.070
So, it really opens up

00:17:45.071 --> 00:17:47.760
the world to be able to
change things and get at

00:17:47.761 --> 00:17:50.490
the power of these ASICs
and really enjoy

00:17:50.491 --> 00:17:54.520
the disaggregation that
the industry is providing.

00:17:55.770 --> 00:18:00.810
So here, this is just the kind of

00:18:00.811 --> 00:18:05.580
network we build to support
our regional networks.

00:18:05.581 --> 00:18:09.215
It's a class style network,

00:18:09.216 --> 00:18:12.330
classes at Bell Labs
mathematician in the 50's that

00:18:12.331 --> 00:18:15.775
figured this stuff out
and did a good job.

00:18:15.776 --> 00:18:20.245
So, there's a lot
of regularity here,

00:18:20.246 --> 00:18:23.830
a lot of power here.

00:18:23.831 --> 00:18:25.710
So, it's like RAID for networks,

00:18:25.711 --> 00:18:27.255
you have lots of components.

00:18:27.256 --> 00:18:28.725
Things can fail, it's okay

00:18:28.726 --> 00:18:30.990
because you've got
more things that are working,

00:18:30.991 --> 00:18:33.690
yet you can have
a full bisection bandwidth.

00:18:33.691 --> 00:18:35.880
Basically, you can
go from any point in

00:18:35.881 --> 00:18:38.970
a region to any other point
without congestion.

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:42.360
You need software running on

00:18:42.361 --> 00:18:45.190
those switches and that's
where we run SONiC.

00:18:47.270 --> 00:18:53.650
So it shows the SONiC
software there.

00:18:53.720 --> 00:19:00.380
Now one test of you're
really managing

00:19:00.381 --> 00:19:06.920
your software like you would
manage software services,

00:19:06.921 --> 00:19:09.010
just stuff works that
you'd expect to work.

00:19:09.011 --> 00:19:12.035
Like down at the bottom,
there's RedisDB.

00:19:12.036 --> 00:19:15.925
It can be any kind of
caching service you want.

00:19:15.926 --> 00:19:19.610
So, in networking, the very

00:19:19.611 --> 00:19:23.235
simplest things like getting
counters out of a box,

00:19:23.236 --> 00:19:25.430
in the old school world there's

00:19:25.431 --> 00:19:28.145
this protocol called SNMP.

00:19:28.146 --> 00:19:29.760
Back in the day, 20 years ago

00:19:29.761 --> 00:19:31.435
when it was invented
it was awesome,

00:19:31.436 --> 00:19:32.720
but now every time you want

00:19:32.721 --> 00:19:35.560
a single column in
SNMP the ITF meets,

00:19:35.561 --> 00:19:37.360
maybe they don't accept
or maybe they do.

00:19:37.361 --> 00:19:39.070
Maybe you beg your vendor
to get it in,

00:19:39.071 --> 00:19:40.900
maybe they do, maybe they don't.

00:19:40.901 --> 00:19:44.210
It's just counters,
what's the big deal?

00:19:44.211 --> 00:19:46.740
So, you can just reflect,

00:19:46.741 --> 00:19:49.010
you can put a Redis agent
on the switch

00:19:49.011 --> 00:19:51.390
and it can just export
whatever counters you need,

00:19:51.391 --> 00:19:54.510
and push-pull,
all those options are yours.

00:19:54.980 --> 00:19:59.780
So, there's the typical
networking applications

00:19:59.781 --> 00:20:02.875
on top of the operating
system, as well as,

00:20:02.876 --> 00:20:06.975
orchestration software and
telemetry software: Ansible,

00:20:06.976 --> 00:20:08.830
which we use, Puppet,

00:20:08.831 --> 00:20:10.820
Chef, and so forth.

00:20:10.821 --> 00:20:16.600
So, we wouldn't do

00:20:16.601 --> 00:20:21.715
this project if it didn't
improve reliability.

00:20:21.716 --> 00:20:24.200
So today, we don't have

00:20:24.201 --> 00:20:27.085
control if we go to
a commercial vendor.

00:20:27.086 --> 00:20:29.205
We're convincing,

00:20:29.206 --> 00:20:30.890
we're building
the world's biggest Cloud,

00:20:30.891 --> 00:20:34.750
we have big pockets,
wallets, or something.

00:20:34.751 --> 00:20:37.480
But we can't control

00:20:37.481 --> 00:20:39.930
the quality of what
goes in that software.

00:20:39.931 --> 00:20:41.450
SONiC gives us that control,

00:20:41.451 --> 00:20:45.695
and then really, it's still
hard work, software's hard.

00:20:45.696 --> 00:20:48.265
But we don't support
every feature,

00:20:48.266 --> 00:20:52.010
we just support what's needed
