00:24:48.470 --> 00:24:54.660
So, this is another slide

00:24:54.661 --> 00:24:57.460
about some of the
challenges that we face.

00:24:57.690 --> 00:25:00.150
If we need a fiber

00:25:00.151 --> 00:25:03.915
nowadays or we need more
capacity between two points,

00:25:03.916 --> 00:25:05.720
we redesign that whole thing.

00:25:05.721 --> 00:25:08.355
We run a boat, we pulled
the fire across the ocean.

00:25:08.356 --> 00:25:12.315
Our team does that.
So, we take on that,

00:25:12.316 --> 00:25:14.600
it's about as routine as

00:25:14.601 --> 00:25:17.100
you might say plug in
an Ethernet cable.

00:25:17.101 --> 00:25:20.795
We run the boat, pull
the fiber, and we're ready.

00:25:20.796 --> 00:25:26.025
But we have to be resilient
to storms, typhoons.

00:25:26.026 --> 00:25:29.475
Fibers somehow gets
the sharks excited,

00:25:29.476 --> 00:25:31.825
they like to bite the fiber.

00:25:31.826 --> 00:25:33.835
There's repeaters down there.

00:25:33.836 --> 00:25:35.710
Sadly, there's
fishing that catches

00:25:35.711 --> 00:25:39.220
the cables and there's
potentially sabotage.

00:25:39.221 --> 00:25:45.890
So, it costs a lot of
money to run the boat out,

00:25:45.891 --> 00:25:47.260
to pull the fiber up, fix it,

00:25:47.261 --> 00:25:49.070
and sink it back down.

00:25:49.071 --> 00:25:52.110
So, we think a lot about

00:25:52.111 --> 00:25:55.890
optimizing for resilient design

00:25:55.891 --> 00:25:58.005
so that we don't have
to rush the boat out.

00:25:58.006 --> 00:26:00.790
We have enough fiber in
the right places that

00:26:00.791 --> 00:26:04.340
it's okay if some of
these disasters happen.

00:26:04.680 --> 00:26:08.245
Netsim is one of the projects
that allows us to

00:26:08.246 --> 00:26:12.250
understand the impact of
any failure on the fiber.

00:26:13.320 --> 00:26:17.050
Okay. So, let me
tell you about that

