my team on how we
built this cable.

00:28:44.040 --> 00:28:49.310
Now, I'm going to talk to
you about the kind of work,

00:28:50.110 --> 00:28:52.610
it's more detailed work.

00:28:52.611 --> 00:28:54.280
The kind of work that happens

00:28:54.281 --> 00:28:55.990
day to day in
operating a network.

00:28:55.991 --> 00:28:58.050
It's not a bad analogy to think

00:28:58.051 --> 00:29:03.910
about flying
an interesting plane,

00:29:04.490 --> 00:29:09.470
except while the plane is flying,

00:29:09.471 --> 00:29:11.900
you're changing the plane
and you're adding engines,

00:29:11.901 --> 00:29:13.410
and taking away engine, and

00:29:13.411 --> 00:29:16.030
the wings and wheels are
also changing and so forth.

00:29:16.031 --> 00:29:18.960
So it's an evolving
plane in flight.

00:29:18.961 --> 00:29:20.910
But the consequences again,

00:29:20.911 --> 00:29:23.045
the reliability, it's
got to be there.

00:29:23.046 --> 00:29:26.180
The reliability
constraints is so strong,

00:29:26.181 --> 00:29:27.570
there is no way that plane can go

00:29:27.571 --> 00:29:30.880
down or even be disrupted.

00:29:31.190 --> 00:29:35.435
We need an emulator, we
need to train our people,

00:29:35.436 --> 00:29:37.850
we need to have the kind
of confidence that

00:29:37.851 --> 00:29:40.350
when they're making
a change to the network,

00:29:40.351 --> 00:29:44.470
and really, people's lives
and work depend on it.

00:29:44.471 --> 00:29:46.235
That it's going to work, and so,

00:29:46.236 --> 00:29:50.180
we need something like
a flight simulator.

00:29:51.730 --> 00:29:54.100
So, we built one, and

00:29:54.101 --> 00:29:55.420
I will tell you
a little bit about it.

00:29:55.421 --> 00:29:57.110
This is the open source thing

00:29:57.111 --> 00:29:59.885
that you can consume
starting in January.

00:29:59.886 --> 00:30:04.310
You can simulate
a network with fidelity,

00:30:04.311 --> 00:30:06.220
you won't be able to tell.

00:30:06.221 --> 00:30:08.810
You can bring in your network
engineer and let them

00:30:08.811 --> 00:30:11.030
go at it and give them
a kind of a touring test.

00:30:11.031 --> 00:30:12.705
Is this the Emulator or
just the the real thing?

00:30:12.706 --> 00:30:14.350
They won't know.

00:30:14.720 --> 00:30:19.830
You can do that with
1,000 devices in minutes.

00:30:19.831 --> 00:30:22.660
It's seamless, it's
easy to deploy,

00:30:22.661 --> 00:30:24.590
and it's again, high-fidelity.

00:30:24.591 --> 00:30:26.880
So, do we use it?

00:30:26.881 --> 00:30:30.570
We spent 12 million core hours
on Azure Network Emulator.

00:30:30.571 --> 00:30:32.420
Before I came here,
we were running it

00:30:32.421 --> 00:30:37.710
for a big network
migration we had to do.

00:30:38.470 --> 00:30:40.700
By the way, because of

00:30:40.701 --> 00:30:42.850
this emulator and
the confidence it gave us,

00:30:42.851 --> 00:30:44.930
we did more network migrations.

00:30:44.931 --> 00:30:48.400
We changed everything
in our network.

00:30:48.401 --> 00:30:50.000
We pulled more cables,

00:30:50.001 --> 00:30:52.230
we changed more routers
and switches,

00:30:52.231 --> 00:30:53.390
we did more stuff to

00:30:53.391 --> 00:30:57.330
the network in the last year
than we've ever done before.

00:30:57.331 --> 00:30:59.670
It was probably
the biggest network migration

00:30:59.671 --> 00:31:02.420
in anybody's history
and nobody noticed.

00:31:02.421 --> 00:31:05.680
But we caught 100 or so bugs
in this Emulator,

00:31:05.681 --> 00:31:07.965
and that's why nobody noticed.

00:31:07.966 --> 00:31:11.250
The bugs never got to production.

00:31:11.251 --> 00:31:14.240
So, the way it works is,

00:31:14.241 --> 00:31:15.620
the network engineer describes

00:31:15.621 --> 00:31:18.240
a change in a
machine-readable form.

00:31:18.241 --> 00:31:20.380
We automatically,

00:31:20.381 --> 00:31:22.925
could pick what vendor
software we want,

00:31:22.926 --> 00:31:26.225
if it's the actual vendor
software and our own software,

00:31:26.226 --> 00:31:31.840
then we download it into
the Emulated Replica and run it.

00:31:31.841 --> 00:31:33.945
We also use theorem
proving technology from

00:31:33.946 --> 00:31:37.175
MSR as well as the emulation,

00:31:37.176 --> 00:31:39.755
and we find out if the intent

00:31:39.756 --> 00:31:43.460
worked as we expected,
the intended change.

00:31:44.580 --> 00:31:51.980
So let me try to
click through this.

00:31:52.130 --> 00:31:57.940
I don't have time
to simulate myself.

00:31:59.810 --> 00:32:08.420
Okay, so one problem you have
with these emulations is,

00:32:08.421 --> 00:32:11.115
how big a network to emulate.

00:32:11.116 --> 00:32:13.330
Where's the boundary? Are you

00:32:13.331 --> 00:32:14.725
going to go for
the whole internet?

00:32:14.726 --> 00:32:16.450
Where do you draw the line?

00:32:16.451 --> 00:32:18.700
Do you have to do
the whole data center?

00:32:18.701 --> 00:32:20.360
Well, you need this fidelity,

00:32:20.361 --> 00:32:23.150
you need to be able to
answer the question that,

00:32:23.151 --> 00:32:24.490
is this change safe?

00:32:24.491 --> 00:32:26.215
Is it going to have
the intended impact?

00:32:26.216 --> 00:32:29.565
So, part of the research
on this had been,

00:32:29.566 --> 00:32:31.580
how do I draw
the smallest boundary?

00:32:31.581 --> 00:32:33.060
So I can answer the question

00:32:33.061 --> 00:32:35.375
without emulating
the entire internet,

00:32:35.376 --> 00:32:37.630
and that's what we do.

00:32:37.631 --> 00:32:43.175
So, we pick the economic
frontier and emulate inside it,

00:32:43.176 --> 00:32:44.655
and we do that all the time,

00:32:44.656 --> 00:32:50.160
and that's described in
an SOSP paper from this year.

00:32:52.690 --> 00:32:57.340
Okay. Let me say
one other thing about it.

00:32:57.341 --> 00:33:02.150
So, again I wanted to tell
you how high-fidelity this,

00:33:02.151 --> 00:33:05.025
if you imagine like
you're an engineer in

00:33:05.026 --> 00:33:07.875
one of these device companies,

00:33:07.876 --> 00:33:12.210
you don't expect your software to

00:33:12.211 --> 00:33:13.920
sort of go to sleep

00:33:13.921 --> 00:33:16.355
and wake up in the
cloud and still work,

00:33:16.356 --> 00:33:19.660
it expects, hey, I've device,

00:33:19.661 --> 00:33:20.950
and I've got these wires,

00:33:20.951 --> 00:33:22.010
and I'm going to
check the health of

00:33:22.011 --> 00:33:24.500
those wires and run
some diagnostics.

00:33:24.501 --> 00:33:26.190
All that has to work.

00:33:26.191 --> 00:33:31.855
It has that software has to
be faked out, and fooled,

00:33:31.856 --> 00:33:35.720
and understand that
the emulated hardware

00:33:35.721 --> 00:33:38.380
is just like the real hardware,

00:33:38.381 --> 00:33:39.620
and that's what happens here.

00:33:39.621 --> 00:33:43.270
So that's part of the interesting
engineering problems.

00:33:43.271 --> 00:33:46.210
Why does that work?

00:33:46.211 --> 00:33:49.230
It's because of the cloud,

00:33:49.231 --> 00:33:50.880
is because of virtual machines,.

00:33:50.881 --> 00:33:52.130
Same deal with virtual machines.

00:33:52.131 --> 00:33:54.850
Virtual machines think
they're on real machines,

00:33:54.851 --> 00:33:56.450
is because of virtual networking.

00:33:56.451 --> 00:33:58.935
So, we had to figure
out all that tech,

00:33:58.936 --> 00:34:00.035
that's why it works now,

00:34:00.036 --> 00:34:02.525
it didn't work five years
ago, or ten years ago.

00:34:02.526 --> 00:34:06.130
We have the mechanisms
because we can

00:34:06.131 --> 00:34:09.740
virtualize everything to
full-fidelity in the cloud.

00:34:09.741 --> 00:34:12.160
We can also virtualize
the networks into this emulator,

00:34:12.161 --> 00:34:13.720
and then use the Cloud to run

00:34:13.721 --> 00:34:16.045
the emulator. That's
why it works.

00:34:16.046 --> 00:34:18.830
So again, try it,

00:34:18.850 --> 00:34:21.970
you can email the developers now

00:34:21.971 --> 00:34:24.380
to get early information about

00:34:24.381 --> 00:34:30.945
it and it will be on the
on Azure market place.

00:34:30.946 --> 00:34:34.975
So this is an open invitation
for all the work.

00:34:34.976 --> 00:34:41.050
Try sonic, try
the emulator. All right.

00:34:41.051 --> 00:34:44.170
I think the appendix,

00:34:44.171 --> 00:34:45.640
which I'm not going to get to,

00:34:45.641 --> 00:34:48.140
has to do with
all those other products

00:34:48.141 --> 00:34:50.210
